]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>The vector u of length 4 along the x-axis and the vector v of length 3 at 30 degrees from it in the xy-plane, with the parallelogram they span shaded, its height 1.5 marked by a dashed segment, and the cross product u cross v of length 6 pointing up the z-axis.</shortdescription>
</image>
</figure>
</sidebyside>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-cross-components">
<title>Second Definition of the Cross Product</title>

<p>
Writing the vectors in components gives another definition for the cross
product.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-cross-components">
<title>The Cross Product (Second Definition)</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>\mathbf u = \langle u_1, u_2, u_3\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \langle v_1, v_2, v_3\rangle</m>, then
<md xml:id="eq-cross-components" number="yes">
\mathbf u \times \mathbf v =
\langle u_2v_3 - u_3v_2,\; u_3v_1 - u_1v_3,\; u_1v_2 - u_2v_1\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
One way to remember the second definition of the cross product is to
write it as a determinant:
<md>
<mrow>
\mathbf u \times \mathbf v \amp =
\begin{vmatrix}
\hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\
u_1 \amp u_2 \amp u_3 \\
v_1 \amp v_2 \amp v_3
\end{vmatrix}
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-cross-determinant" number="yes">
\amp =
\begin{vmatrix} u_2 \amp u_3 \\ v_2 \amp v_3 \end{vmatrix}\hat{\imath}
- \begin{vmatrix} u_1 \amp u_3 \\ v_1 \amp v_3 \end{vmatrix}\hat{\jmath}
+ \begin{vmatrix} u_1 \amp u_2 \\ v_1 \amp v_2 \end{vmatrix}\hat{k},
</mrow>
</md>
where
<md xml:id="eq-two-by-two-determinant" number="yes">
\begin{vmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.
</md>
</p>

<example xml:id="example-cross-compute">
<title>Computing a cross product</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the cross product <m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> if
<m>\mathbf u = \langle 1,-1,2\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \langle 3,-2,1\rangle</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
By <xref ref="eq-cross-determinant"/> and
<xref ref="eq-two-by-two-determinant"/>,
<md>
<mrow>
\mathbf u \times \mathbf v \amp=
\begin{vmatrix}
\hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\
1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
3 \amp -2 \amp 1
\end{vmatrix}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp=
\begin{vmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ -2 \amp 1 \end{vmatrix}\hat{\imath}
- \begin{vmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 1 \end{vmatrix}\hat{\jmath}
+ \begin{vmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \\ 3 \amp -2 \end{vmatrix}\hat{k}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= (-1+4)\hat{\imath} - (1-6)\hat{\jmath} + (-2+3)\hat{k}
= 3\hat{\imath} + 5\hat{\jmath} + \hat{k}
= \langle 3,5,1\rangle.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-perpendicular-vector">
<title>Computing a vector perpendicular to two other vectors</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the points <m>A(1,-1,2)</m>, <m>B(2,0,-1)</m>, and
<m>C(0,-2,3)</m>. Find a unit vector which is orthogonal to both
<m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{AC}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We first compute the two vectors:
<md>
<mrow>\overrightarrow{AB} \amp= \langle 1,1,-3\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\overrightarrow{AC} \amp= \langle -1,-1,1\rangle.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
The cross product <m>\overrightarrow{AB} \times \overrightarrow{AC}</m> is orthogonal to both
vectors <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{AC}</m>:
<md>
<mrow>
\overrightarrow{AB} \times \overrightarrow{AC} \amp=
\begin{vmatrix}
\hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\
1 \amp 1 \amp -3 \\
-1 \amp -1 \amp 1
\end{vmatrix}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp=
\begin{vmatrix} 1 \amp -3 \\ -1 \amp 1 \end{vmatrix}\hat{\imath}
- \begin{vmatrix} 1 \amp -3 \\ -1 \amp 1 \end{vmatrix}\hat{\jmath}
+ \begin{vmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp -1 \end{vmatrix}\hat{k}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= (1-3)\hat{\imath} - (1-3)\hat{\jmath} + (-1+1)\hat{k}
= -2\hat{\imath} + 2\hat{\jmath}
= \langle -2,2,0\rangle.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
The unit vector in question is then computed as follows:
<md xml:id="eq-perpendicular-unit">
       \begin{aligned}
\hat{n} \amp = \frac{\overrightarrow{AB} \times \overrightarrow{AC}}{\left\|\overrightarrow{AB} \times \overrightarrow{AC}\right\|}\\
\amp = \frac{1}{\sqrt 8}\langle -2,2,0\rangle
= \left\langle -\frac{1}{\sqrt 2},\frac{1}{\sqrt 2},0\right\rangle
= -\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\imath} + \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\jmath}.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-normal-vector-video">
<caption>
The unit vector <m>\hat{n}</m> is orthogonal to both
<m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{AC}</m>, and hence to the plane through the
points <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-normal-vector"
       source="videos/normal_vector_3d_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/normal_vector_3d_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-triangle-area">
<title>Computing the area of a triangle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the area of the triangle with the vertices <m>A(1,-1,2)</m>,
<m>B(2,0,-1)</m>, and <m>C(0,-2,3)</m>. Note that these are the same
points that we had in the previous example.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
To find the area of the triangle, we first compute the area of the
parallelogram built on <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{AC}</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-triangle-area"/>, and then divide by <m>2</m>. By
<xref ref="eq-parallelogram-area"/>,
<md>
<mrow>
\text{Area of the parallelogram} \amp=
\left\|\overrightarrow{AB} \times \overrightarrow{AC}\right\| = \sqrt 8 = 2\sqrt 2
</mrow>
<mrow>
\text{Area of the triangle} \amp=
\frac12 \left\|\overrightarrow{AB} \times \overrightarrow{AC}\right\| = \sqrt 2.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-triangle-area">
<caption>
The triangle built on the vectors <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{AC}</m>
is half of the corresponding parallelogram.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-triangle-area">
<diagram dimensions="(310,200)" margins="10">
<definition>b=(4,0)</definition>
<definition>c=(1.5,3)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-0.6,6,3.6)">
<line at="tri-side-bc" endpoints="(c, b)" stroke="black" dash="4 4"/>
<line at="tri-side-top" endpoints="(c, b+c)" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
<line at="tri-side-right" endpoints="(b, b+c)" stroke="blue" dash="4 4"/>
<vector at="tri-vec-ac" v="c" tail="(0,0)" stroke="blue"/>
<vector at="tri-vec-ab" v="b" tail="(0,0)" stroke="red"/>
<label at="tri-ac-label" anchor="(0.75,1.5)" alignment="nw" color="blue">
<m>\overrightarrow{AC}</m>
</label>
<label at="tri-ab-label" anchor="(2,0)" alignment="s" color="red">
<m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations text="A triangle formed by the vectors AB and AC drawn tail to tail from the vertex A, together with a dashed segment from the tip of AC to the tip of AB. Two more dashed segments complete the parallelogram built on AB and AC, showing that the triangle is half of the parallelogram.">
<annotation ref="tri-vec-ab" text="Vector AB, along the horizontal direction."/>
<annotation ref="tri-vec-ac" text="Vector AC, drawn from the same tail as AB."/>
<annotation ref="tri-side-bc" text="The dashed third side of the triangle, from the tip of AC to the tip of AB."/>
<annotation ref="tri-side-top" text="A dashed side completing the parallelogram, parallel to AB."/>
<annotation ref="tri-side-right" text="A dashed side completing the parallelogram, parallel to AC."/>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-true-false">
<title>True or False?</title>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-true-false-1">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="yes">
<p>
Let <m>\overrightarrow{u}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m> be two vectors in
<m>\mathbb R^3</m>. Then, <m>\overrightarrow{u} \times \text{proj}_{\overrightarrow{v}}\overrightarrow{u}</m>
is perpendicular to <m>\overrightarrow{u} - \text{proj}_{\overrightarrow{v}}\overrightarrow{u}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Note that <m>\overrightarrow{u} \times \text{proj}_{\overrightarrow{v}}\overrightarrow{u}</m> is perpendicular to both
<m>\overrightarrow{u}</m> and <m>\text{proj}_{\overrightarrow{v}}\overrightarrow{u}</m>, which gives:
<md xml:id="eq-true-false-1">
<mrow>
\left(\overrightarrow{u} \times \text{proj}_{\overrightarrow{v}}\overrightarrow{u}\right) \cdot
\left(\overrightarrow{u} - \text{proj}_{\overrightarrow{v}}\overrightarrow{u}\right)
\amp =
\left(\overrightarrow{u} \times \text{proj}_{\overrightarrow{v}}\overrightarrow{u}\right) \cdot \overrightarrow{u}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad - \left(\overrightarrow{u} \times \text{proj}_{\overrightarrow{v}}\overrightarrow{u}\right)
 \cdot \text{proj}_{\overrightarrow{v}}\overrightarrow{u}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = 0 - 0 = 0,
</mrow>
</md>
and therefore <m>\overrightarrow{u} \times \text{proj}_{\overrightarrow{v}}\overrightarrow{u}</m> is
perpendicular to <m>\overrightarrow{u} - \text{proj}_{\overrightarrow{v}}\overrightarrow{u}</m>.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-true-false-2">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="yes">
<p>
Let <m>\hat{u}</m> and <m>\hat{v}</m> be two standard unit vectors in
<m>\mathbb R^3</m>. Then,
<m>\left(\hat{u} \cdot \hat{v}\right)^2 + \left\|\hat{v} \times \hat{u}\right\|^2 = 1</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Note that:
<md>
<mrow>
\left(\hat{u} \cdot \hat{v}\right)^2 + \left\|\hat{v} \times \hat{u}\right\|^2
\amp= \|\hat{u}\|^2\|\hat{v}\|^2\cos^2\theta + \|\hat{u}\|^2\|\hat{v}\|^2\sin^2\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \|\hat{u}\|^2\|\hat{v}\|^2\left(\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta\right)
= \|\hat{u}\|^2\|\hat{v}\|^2.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Now, since <m>\hat{u}</m> and <m>\hat{v}</m> are standard unit vectors, we
have <m>\|\hat{u}\| = \|\hat{v}\| = 1</m>, which means:
<md xml:id="eq-true-false-3">
\left(\hat{u} \cdot \hat{v}\right)^2 + \left\|\hat{v} \times \hat{u}\right\|^2
= \|\hat{u}\|^2\|\hat{v}\|^2 = 1.
</md>
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-torque">
<title>*Application: Torque</title>

<p>
<em>Starred section.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>

<p>
One of the well known physical applications of the cross product is
<term>torque</term>, the rotational analog of force. A force makes an
object accelerate; a torque makes it <em>rotate</em>. How effective a
force is at producing rotation depends not only on its magnitude, but
also on <em>where</em> it is applied and in <em>what direction</em>.
Think of pushing a door: pushing hard near the hinge barely moves it,
while a light push at the doorknob swings it open easily. Pushing at the
doorknob but <em>toward</em> the hinge does nothing at all. The cross
product captures all three scenarios and more.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-torque">
<title>Torque</title>
<statement>
<p>
Suppose a force <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> is applied at a point whose position
vector relative to the axis of rotation is <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>. The
<term>torque</term> produced by <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> is the vector
<md xml:id="eq-torque-def" number="yes">
\overrightarrow{\tau} = \overrightarrow{r} \times \overrightarrow{F}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<figure xml:id="fig-int-torque">
<caption>
Interactive view of <xref ref="eq-torque-def"/>. The force <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> is
applied at the head of <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>, and the torque
<m>\overrightarrow{\tau} = \overrightarrow{r} \times \overrightarrow{F}</m> points along the axis of rotation.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-torque" iframe="torque-cross-product.html" width="100%" aspect="8:7">
<instructions>
<p>
In the left pane the axis of rotation passes through the marked point,
perpendicular to the screen. Drag the head of the yellow position vector
<m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> to move the point where the force is applied, and drag the
head of the red force <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> to change its size and direction. The
dashed red line is the line along which the force acts, and the violet
segment is the lever arm <m>r_\perp = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|\sin\theta</m>, the
perpendicular distance from the axis to that line. The shaded
parallelogram built on <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> has area
<m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\|</m>, and the curved arrow shows the sense of the rotation
the torque produces.
</p>
<p>
The right pane shows the same two vectors in space, together with
<m>\overrightarrow{\tau} = \overrightarrow{r} \times \overrightarrow{F}</m> along the axis; drag that pane to
view it from another angle, or press <q>view</q> to restore the original
one. Press <q>animate</q> to swing <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> once around the point of
application: watch <m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\|</m> reach its largest value when
<m>\overrightarrow{F} \perp \overrightarrow{r}</m>, fall to zero when <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> points
straight at (or away from) the axis, and reverse direction as the
rotation changes sense. Either vector can also be moved with the keyboard
(press <q>Tab</q> to reach its head, then use the arrow keys).
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<p>
Everything we know about the cross product now translates into physics.
By <xref ref="eq-cross-geometric"/>, the magnitude of the torque is
<md xml:id="eq-torque-magnitude" number="yes">
\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|\|\overrightarrow{F}\||\sin\theta|,
</md>

where <m>\theta</m> is the angle between <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> and
<m>\overrightarrow{F}</m>, and the direction of <m>\overrightarrow{\tau}</m> is given by the
right-hand rule: <m>\overrightarrow{\tau}</m> points along the axis of rotation, and
curling the fingers of the right hand from <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> toward
<m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> gives the sense of the rotation. Torque has units of
meter-newtons (<m>\text{m}\cdot\text{N}</m>) in SI.
</p>

<p>
The quantity in <xref ref="eq-torque-magnitude"/> can be grouped in two
useful ways:
<md>
<mrow>
\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| \amp= \underbrace{\left(\|\overrightarrow{r}\|\sin\theta\right)}_{r_\perp}\|\overrightarrow{F}\|
 = r_\perp \|\overrightarrow{F}\|,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| \amp= \|\overrightarrow{r}\|\underbrace{\left(\|\overrightarrow{F}\|\sin\theta\right)}_{F_\perp}
 = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|\, F_\perp.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-torque-two-groupings">
<caption>
The two groupings of <xref ref="eq-torque-magnitude"/>. In both pictures the
axis of rotation passes through <m>O</m>, perpendicular to the page, the
force <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> is applied at <m>P</m>, and <m>\theta</m> is the angle
between <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m>; the two products are equal because
each is <m>\|\overrightarrow{r}\|\|\overrightarrow{F}\|\sin\theta</m>.
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="2%">

<figure xml:id="fig-torque-lever-arm">
<caption>
Force times lever arm: <m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| = r_\perp\|\overrightarrow{F}\|</m>.
</caption>
<image width="100%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-torque-lever-arm">
<diagram dimensions="(330,300)" margins="8">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.1,-3.95,6.5,2.95)">

<line at="la-action" p1="(2.451,-2.212)" p2="(5.721,2.457)"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="1.4" dash="5 4"/>
<line at="la-rext" p1="(4,0)" p2="(6.0,0)"
      stroke="#0072B2" thickness="1.2" dash="3 4"/>

<line at="la-lever" endpoints="((0,0), (2.684,-1.879))"
      stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>

<vector at="la-r" v="(4,0)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="la-F" v="(1.491,2.130)" tail="(4,0)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>

<angle-marker at="la-theta" points="((5.6,0),(4,0),(5.491,2.130))"
              radius="30" stroke="black"><m>\theta</m></angle-marker>
<angle-marker at="la-right" points="((5.721,2.457),(2.684,-1.879),(0,0))"
              radius="13" stroke="#009E73"/>

<point at="la-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>
<point at="la-P" p="(4,0)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>
<point at="la-Q" p="(2.684,-1.879)" fill="#009E73" radius="3"/>

<label at="la-Ol" anchor="(-0.12,-0.10)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
<label at="la-Pl" anchor="(4.02,0.10)" alignment="nw"><m>P</m></label>
<label at="la-rl" anchor="(2.0,0.08)" alignment="n" color="#0072B2">
<m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>
</label>
<label at="la-Fl" anchor="(5.30,2.35)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>\overrightarrow{F}</m>
</label>
<label at="la-leverl" anchor="(1.22,-0.86)" alignment="ne" color="#009E73">
<m>r_\perp</m>
</label>
<label at="la-eq1" anchor="(2.75,-3.05)" alignment="c">
<m>r_\perp = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|\sin\theta</m>
</label>
<label at="la-eq2" anchor="(2.75,-3.75)" alignment="c">
<m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| = r_\perp\|\overrightarrow{F}\|</m>
</label>

</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The axis of rotation passes through the point O, perpendicular to the page, and the force F is applied at the point P at the head of the position vector r. The line along which the force acts is extended backwards past P as a dashed line, and it passes below O. The perpendicular segment from O to that line is the lever arm r perpendicular, whose length is the length of r times the sine of theta. The torque is the length of the force times this lever arm.">
  <annotation ref="la-r" text="The position vector r, drawn from the axis at O to the point of application P."/>
  <annotation ref="la-F" text="The force F, drawn from P, making an angle theta with the direction of r."/>
  <annotation ref="la-action" text="The dashed line along which the force acts, extended in both directions past P."/>
  <annotation ref="la-lever" text="The lever arm, the perpendicular segment from the axis at O to the line of action of the force."/>
  <annotation ref="la-right" text="A right angle, showing that the lever arm meets the line of action perpendicularly."/>
  <annotation ref="la-theta" text="The angle theta at P, between the direction of r and the force F."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-torque-perp-force">
<caption>
Distance times perpendicular force:
<m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|F_\perp</m>.
</caption>
<image width="100%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-torque-perp-force">
<diagram dimensions="(330,300)" margins="8">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.1,-3.95,6.5,2.95)">

<line at="fp-boxv" p1="(5.491,0)" p2="(5.491,2.130)"
      stroke="#999999" thickness="1.2" dash="3 4"/>
<line at="fp-boxh" p1="(4,2.130)" p2="(5.491,2.130)"
      stroke="#999999" thickness="1.2" dash="3 4"/>

<vector at="fp-r" v="(4,0)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="fp-Fpar" v="(1.491,0)" tail="(4,0)" stroke="#666666" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="fp-Fperp" v="(0,2.130)" tail="(4,0)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="fp-F" v="(1.491,2.130)" tail="(4,0)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>

<angle-marker at="fp-theta" points="((5.6,0),(4,0),(5.491,2.130))"
              radius="30" stroke="black"><m>\theta</m></angle-marker>
<angle-marker at="fp-right" points="((5.6,0),(4,0),(4,2.130))"
              radius="13" stroke="#009E73"/>

<point at="fp-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>
<point at="fp-P" p="(4,0)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>

<label at="fp-Ol" anchor="(-0.12,-0.10)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
<label at="fp-Pl" anchor="(3.94,-0.10)" alignment="sw"><m>P</m></label>
<label at="fp-rl" anchor="(2.0,0.08)" alignment="n" color="#0072B2">
<m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>
</label>
<label at="fp-Fl" anchor="(5.55,2.20)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>\overrightarrow{F}</m>
</label>
<label at="fp-Fperpl" anchor="(3.90,1.15)" alignment="w" color="#009E73">
<m>F_\perp</m>
</label>
<label at="fp-Fparl" anchor="(4.75,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#666666">
<m>F_\parallel</m>
</label>
<label at="fp-note" anchor="(2.75,-1.95)" alignment="c" color="#666666">
<m>F_\parallel \text{ produces no rotation}</m>
</label>
<label at="fp-eq1" anchor="(2.75,-3.05)" alignment="c">
<m>F_\perp = \|\overrightarrow{F}\|\sin\theta</m>
</label>
<label at="fp-eq2" anchor="(2.75,-3.75)" alignment="c">
<m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|F_\perp</m>
</label>

</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The same position vector r from the axis at O to the point of application P, with the force F at P split into two pieces by a dashed rectangle. One piece is perpendicular to r and has length the length of F times the sine of theta; the other piece is parallel to r and points straight away from the axis. The torque is the length of r times the perpendicular piece; the parallel piece produces no rotation.">
  <annotation ref="fp-r" text="The position vector r, drawn from the axis at O to the point of application P."/>
  <annotation ref="fp-F" text="The force F, drawn from P, making an angle theta with the direction of r."/>
  <annotation ref="fp-Fperp" text="The component of the force perpendicular to r, of length F perpendicular; this is the part that turns the object."/>
  <annotation ref="fp-Fpar" text="The component of the force parallel to r, pointing straight away from the axis; this part produces no rotation."/>
  <annotation ref="fp-right" text="A right angle between the two components of the force."/>
  <annotation ref="fp-theta" text="The angle theta at P, between the direction of r and the force F."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</sidebyside>
</figure>

<p>
The first grouping, shown in <xref ref="fig-torque-lever-arm"/>, says the
torque is the force times the
<term>lever arm</term> <m>r_\perp</m>, the perpendicular distance from
the axis to the line along which the force acts. The second, shown in
<xref ref="fig-torque-perp-force"/>, says it is
the distance <m>\|\overrightarrow{r}\|</m> times <m>F_\perp</m>, the component of the
force perpendicular to <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>; the parallel component
<m>F_\parallel</m> points straight at the axis and produces no rotation.
</p>

<fact xml:id="fact-zero-torque">
<title>Zero Torque</title>
<statement>
<p>
By <xref ref="eq-parallel-vectors"/>, if <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> is parallel to
<m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> then <m>\overrightarrow{\tau} = \overrightarrow{r} \times \overrightarrow{F} = \overrightarrow{0}</m>: a
force directed straight at (or away from) the axis produces no rotation.
This is why you cannot open a door by pushing toward its hinge.
</p>
</statement>
</fact>

<example xml:id="example-biceps-torque">
<title>Biceps torque</title>
<statement>
<p>
The biceps muscle exerts a vertical force of
<m>\|\overrightarrow{F}\| = 700</m> N on the lower arm, attached at a point
<m>5.0</m> cm from the elbow joint (the axis of rotation). Calculate
the torque about the elbow when
</p>
<p>
<ol>
<li><p>the forearm is horizontal, and</p></li>
<li><p>the forearm makes a <m>30^\circ</m> angle below the horizontal.</p></li>
</ol>
</p>
<!-- Hidden: the biceps torque video. Nothing cross-references it, so it can
     be restored by simply removing this comment wrapper.
<figure xml:id="fig-biceps-torque-video">
<caption>
The torque <m>\overrightarrow{\tau} = \overrightarrow{r} \times \overrightarrow{F}</m> of the biceps force
about the elbow. When the forearm drops <m>30^\circ</m> below the
horizontal, the angle between <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> becomes
<m>120^\circ</m>, the lever arm <m>r_\perp = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|\sin\theta</m>
shortens, and the torque decreases from <m>35</m> to <m>30</m>
<m>\text{m}\cdot\text{N}</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-biceps-torque"
       source="videos/biceps_torque_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/biceps_torque_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
-->

<figure xml:id="fig-biceps-torque">
  <caption>The biceps exerts a 700 N force on the forearm: (a) forearm horizontal, with the muscle attached 5.0 cm from the elbow axis; (b) forearm at 30&#xb0; below horizontal, with lever arm <m>R</m>.</caption>
  <image source="images/biceps-side-by-side.png" width="100%">
    <description>
<p>
Side-by-side diagrams of an arm. In (a), the forearm is horizontal and a 700 N biceps force acts 5.0 cm from the elbow axis. In (b), the forearm is angled 30 degrees below horizontal, making a 60 degree angle with the vertical, with lever arm R.
</p>
</description>
  </image>
</figure>
</statement>

<solution>

<p>
Place the elbow at the origin and let the force act in the plane
<m>z = 0</m>, with <m>\overrightarrow{F} = \langle 0, 700, 0\rangle</m> pointing
straight up.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part 1.</em> The forearm is horizontal, so
<m>\overrightarrow{r} = \langle 0.050, 0, 0\rangle</m> and the angle between
<m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> is <m>\theta = 90^\circ</m>. Then
<md>
<mrow>
\overrightarrow{\tau} = \overrightarrow{r} \times \overrightarrow{F} \amp=
\begin{vmatrix}
\hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\
0.050 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 700 \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \langle 0, 0, (0.050)(700)\rangle
= \langle 0, 0, 35 \rangle,
</mrow>
</md>
so <m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| = 35\ \text{m}\cdot\text{N}</m>. Equivalently,
<m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|\|\overrightarrow{F}\|\sin 90^\circ = (0.050)(700)(1) = 35\
\text{m}\cdot\text{N}</m>. The torque points in the direction
<m>+\hat{k}</m> (out of the page), which by the right-hand rule
corresponds to a counterclockwise rotation of the forearm about the
elbow.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part 2.</em> Now the forearm points <m>30^\circ</m> below the
horizontal, so
<md xml:id="eq-biceps-r-vector" number="yes">
\overrightarrow{r} = 0.050\,\langle \cos(-30^\circ), \sin(-30^\circ), 0\rangle
       = \langle 0.0433, -0.025, 0\rangle,
</md>

while <m>\overrightarrow{F} = \langle 0, 700, 0\rangle</m> is unchanged. The cross
product gives
<md>
<mrow>
\overrightarrow{\tau} = \overrightarrow{r} \times \overrightarrow{F} \amp=
\begin{vmatrix}
\hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\
0.0433 \amp -0.025 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 700 \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \langle 0, 0, (0.0433)(700) - (-0.025)(0)\rangle
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \langle 0, 0, 30.3 \rangle \approx \langle 0, 0, 30 \rangle.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
So <m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| \approx 30\ \text{m}\cdot\text{N}</m>, again
counterclockwise. To check with <xref ref="eq-torque-magnitude"/>: the
vector <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> sits at <m>-30^\circ</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> at
<m>90^\circ</m>, so the angle between them is <m>\theta = 120^\circ</m>,
and
<md xml:id="eq-biceps-check" number="yes">
\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|\|\overrightarrow{F}\|\sin 120^\circ
= (0.050)(700)\left(\tfrac{\sqrt 3}{2}\right)
\approx 30\ \text{m}\cdot\text{N}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Note that <m>\sin 120^\circ = \sin 60^\circ</m>, so this agrees with
the lever-arm point of view: the lever arm has shortened to
<m>r_\perp = (0.050)\sin 60^\circ \approx 0.043</m> m. The arm exerts
less torque at this angle than when the forearm is horizontal —
weight machines at gyms are often designed to account for exactly this
variation.
</p>

</solution>
</example>


</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-circular-motion">
<title>*Application: Circular Motion: Velocity and Angular Velocity</title>

<introduction>
<p>
<em>Starred section.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>
<p>
A second physical application of the cross product describes the motion of
an object travelling in a circle: a rider on a circular track, a ball whirled
on a string, a point on a spinning wheel, or the moon on its (nearly
circular) orbit. In each case the object turns about a fixed centre, and the
natural way to keep track of where it is at time <m>t</m> is not by its
coordinates <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> but by the single angle <m>\theta(t)</m>
it has swung through. Our goal is to study how the velocity and the rate of
change of that angle are related.
</p>

<p>
Throughout this section we write unit vectors with hats, so that
<m>\hat{\imath}</m>, <m>\hat{\jmath}</m> and <m>\hat{k}</m> are the usual fixed unit
vectors <m>\hat{\imath}</m>, <m>\hat{\jmath}</m> and <m>\hat{k}</m> along the
coordinate axes.
</p>
</introduction>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-polar-frame">
<title>A Frame that Turns with the Particle</title>

<p>
Let an object move on a circle of radius <m>r</m> centred at the origin of
the <m>xy</m>-plane, and let <m>\theta(t)</m> be the angle its position
makes with the positive <m>x</m>-axis at time <m>t</m>, as in
<xref ref="fig-polar-frame"/>. At the point <m>P</m> where the particle sits
we attach two unit vectors:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>\hat r(t)</m>, pointing radially outward, away from the centre, and
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\hat\theta(t)</m>, perpendicular to <m>\hat r(t)</m> and tangent to the
circle, pointing in the direction of increasing <m>\theta</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
Reading their components off the figure,
<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-rhat" number="yes">\hat r(t) \amp = \cos\theta(t)\,\hat{\imath} + \sin\theta(t)\,\hat{\jmath},</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-thetahat" number="yes">\hat\theta(t) \amp = -\sin\theta(t)\,\hat{\imath} + \cos\theta(t)\,\hat{\jmath}.</mrow>
</md>
The position of the particle is then simply
<md xml:id="eq-position-polar" number="yes">
\overrightarrow{r}(t) = r\,\hat r(t).
</md>
</p>

<p>
The pair <m>\hat{\imath}</m>, <m>\hat{\jmath}</m> is fixed once and for all, while the
pair <m>\hat r(t)</m>, <m>\hat\theta(t)</m> rides along with the particle and
turns as it goes; this is exactly what makes the second pair convenient. Note
that together with <m>\hat{k}</m> the moving pair forms a right-handed frame,
since
<md>
<mrow>
\hat r \times \hat\theta \amp =
\begin{vmatrix}
\hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\
\cos\theta \amp \sin\theta \amp 0 \\
-\sin\theta \amp \cos\theta \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-polar-frame-right-handed" number="yes">
\amp = \left(\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta\right)\hat{k} = \hat{k}.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-polar-frame">
<caption>
The moving frame at the point <m>P</m>: <m>\hat r(t)</m> points radially
outward and <m>\hat\theta(t)</m> is tangent to the circle. The fixed vectors
<m>\hat{\imath}</m> and <m>\hat{\jmath}</m> are drawn at <m>P</m> for comparison; unlike
<m>\hat r</m> and <m>\hat\theta</m>, they do not turn as the particle moves.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<description>
<p>
A circle of radius r centred at the origin. A particle sits at a point P on
the circle, its position vector making an angle theta of t with the positive
x-axis. At P the unit vector r hat points radially outward along the
position vector, and the unit vector theta hat is perpendicular to it,
tangent to the circle in the counterclockwise direction. The fixed unit
vectors i and j are also drawn at P for comparison.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-polar-frame">
      <diagram dimensions="(455,430)" margins="14">
      <coordinates bbox="(-3.5,-3.1,3.9,3.9)">

      <line at="pf-ax" p1="(-3.15,0)" p2="(3.35,0)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4"/>
      <line at="pf-ay" p1="(0,-2.75)" p2="(0,3.35)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4"/>

      <circle at="pf-circle" center="(0,0)" radius="2.4"
              stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="2" fill="none"/>

      <vector at="pf-r" tail="(0,0)" v="(1.5427,1.8384)" stroke="#666666" thickness="2"/>

      <vector at="pf-i" tail="(1.5427,1.8384)" v="(1,0)" stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="2"/>
      <vector at="pf-j" tail="(1.5427,1.8384)" v="(0,1)" stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="2"/>

      <vector at="pf-rhat" tail="(1.5427,1.8384)" v="(0.6428,0.766)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="pf-thetahat" tail="(1.5427,1.8384)" v="(-0.766,0.6428)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>

      <angle-marker font-size="18" at="pf-theta" points="((1,0),(0,0),(0.6428,0.766))"
                    radius="42" stroke="black"><m>\theta(t)</m></angle-marker>

      <point at="pf-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3"/>
      <point at="pf-P" p="(1.5427,1.8384)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>

      <label font-size="18" at="pf-Pl" anchor="(1.60,1.76)" alignment="se"><m>P</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="pf-rl" anchor="(0.66,1.00)" alignment="nw" color="#666666"><m>r</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="pf-rhl" anchor="(2.24,2.66)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
      <m>\hat r(t)</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="pf-thl" anchor="(0.72,2.54)" alignment="nw" color="#009E73">
      <m>\hat\theta(t)</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="pf-il" anchor="(2.60,1.80)" alignment="se" color="#5B9BD5"><m>\hat{\imath}</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="pf-jl" anchor="(1.60,2.88)" alignment="e" color="#5B9BD5"><m>\hat{\jmath}</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="pf-xl" anchor="(3.25,0.05)" alignment="ne"><m>+x</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="pf-yl" anchor="(0.10,3.25)" alignment="e"><m>+y</m></label>

      </coordinates>

      <annotations>
      <annotation ref="figure" text="A circle of radius r centred at the origin. A particle sits at a point P on the circle, its position vector making an angle theta of t with the positive x axis. At P the unit vector r hat points radially outward along the position vector and the unit vector theta hat is perpendicular to it, tangent to the circle in the counterclockwise direction. The fixed unit vectors i and j are also drawn at P for comparison.">
        <annotation ref="pf-circle" text="The circular orbit of radius r."/>
        <annotation ref="pf-r" text="The position vector of the particle, of length r."/>
        <annotation ref="pf-rhat" text="The unit vector r hat, pointing radially outward from the centre."/>
        <annotation ref="pf-thetahat" text="The unit vector theta hat, tangent to the circle, perpendicular to r hat."/>
        <annotation ref="pf-theta" text="The angle theta of t between the positive x axis and the position vector."/>
      </annotation>
      </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-circular-velocity">
<title>The Velocity in Circular Motion</title>

<p>
Because the particle moves, the angle <m>\theta</m> depends on <m>t</m>, and
so do the two moving unit vectors. Differentiating <xref ref="eq-rhat"/> and
<xref ref="eq-thetahat"/> with the chain rule gives
<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-drhat" number="yes">\frac{d\hat r}{dt} \amp = \left(-\sin\theta\,\hat{\imath}
  + \cos\theta\,\hat{\jmath}\right)\frac{d\theta}{dt}
  = \frac{d\theta}{dt}\,\hat\theta,</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-dthetahat" number="yes">\frac{d\hat\theta}{dt} \amp = \left(-\cos\theta\,\hat{\imath}
  - \sin\theta\,\hat{\jmath}\right)\frac{d\theta}{dt}
  = -\frac{d\theta}{dt}\,\hat r.</mrow>
</md>
Each unit vector has constant length, so it can only turn; and turning a
unit vector moves its tip perpendicular to itself, which is exactly what
<xref ref="eq-drhat"/> and <xref ref="eq-dthetahat"/> say.
</p>

<p>
Now differentiate the position <xref ref="eq-position-polar"/>. The radius
<m>r</m> is constant on a circle, so only the unit vector needs
differentiating:
<md xml:id="eq-circular-velocity" number="yes">
\overrightarrow{v}(t) = \frac{d\overrightarrow{r}}{dt} = r\,\frac{d\hat r}{dt}
 = r\,\frac{d\theta}{dt}\,\hat\theta(t).
</md>
The velocity has no <m>\hat r</m>-component at all: it points purely along
<m>\hat\theta</m>, tangent to the circle. Its <m>\hat\theta</m>-component
<md xml:id="eq-tangential-velocity" number="yes">
v_\theta = r\,\frac{d\theta}{dt}
</md>
is called the <term>tangential component of the velocity</term>. It is
positive when the particle turns counterclockwise and negative when it turns
clockwise.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-angular-speed">
<title>Angular Speed</title>
<statement>
<p>
The <term>angular speed</term> of the particle is the magnitude of the rate
of change of its angle,
<md xml:id="eq-angular-speed" number="yes">
\omega = \left|\frac{d\theta}{dt}\right|,
</md>
measured in radians per second. By <xref ref="eq-circular-velocity"/>, the
speed of the particle and its angular speed are related by
<md xml:id="eq-speed-omega-r" number="yes">
v = \left\|\overrightarrow{v}\right\| = r\,\omega.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
It is worth seeing where <xref ref="eq-speed-omega-r"/> comes from
geometrically, without any differentiation of unit vectors. In a short time
interval <m>\Delta t</m> the particle moves from <m>\overrightarrow{r}(t)</m> to
<m>\overrightarrow{r}(t+\Delta t)</m>, turning through a small angle
<m>\Delta\theta</m>, as in <xref ref="fig-circular-chord"/>. The two
position vectors have the same length <m>r</m>, so they form an isosceles
triangle whose third side is the displacement
<m>\Delta\overrightarrow{r} = \overrightarrow{r}(t+\Delta t) - \overrightarrow{r}(t)</m>. Splitting that triangle
down the middle gives
<md xml:id="eq-chord-length" number="yes">
\left\|\Delta\overrightarrow{r}\right\| = 2r\sin\frac{\Delta\theta}{2}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
For a small angle the power series
<m>\sin x = x - \frac{x^3}{3!} + \frac{x^5}{5!} - \cdots</m> is dominated by
its first term, so <m>\sin(\Delta\theta/2)\approx \Delta\theta/2</m> and the
chord is very nearly the arc it cuts off:
<m>\left\|\Delta\overrightarrow{r}\right\| \approx r\,\Delta\theta</m>. Dividing by
<m>\Delta t</m> and letting <m>\Delta t \to 0</m>,
<md>
<mrow>
v \amp = \lim_{\Delta t\to 0}\frac{\left\|\Delta\overrightarrow{r}\right\|}{\Delta t}
 = r\lim_{\Delta t\to 0}\frac{\left|\Delta\theta\right|}{\Delta t}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = r\left|\frac{d\theta}{dt}\right| = r\,\omega,
</mrow>
</md>
which is <xref ref="eq-speed-omega-r"/> again. The direction comes out of the
same picture: as <m>\Delta t \to 0</m> the chord <m>\Delta\overrightarrow{r}</m> lines up
with the tangent line to the circle, so <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m> is perpendicular to
<m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>, in agreement with <xref ref="eq-circular-velocity"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-circular-chord">
<caption>
The displacement <m>\Delta\overrightarrow{r}</m> is the chord subtending the angle
<m>\Delta\theta</m>. For small <m>\Delta\theta</m> the chord is nearly the
arc, <m>\left\|\Delta\overrightarrow{r}\right\| \approx r\,\Delta\theta</m>, and its
direction approaches that of the tangent line, along which <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m>
points.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
Two position vectors of equal length r are drawn from the centre of a dashed
circle, separated by a small angle delta theta. The chord delta r joins the
tip of the first to the tip of the second. A dashed tangent line touches the
circle at the first point, and the velocity arrow at that point lies along
it.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-circular-chord">
      <diagram dimensions="(470,492)" margins="14">
      <coordinates bbox="(-3.7,-4.25,3.7,3.5)">

      <circle at="ch-circle" center="(0,0)" radius="2.2"
              stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="1.8" fill="none" dash="4 3"/>

      <line at="ch-tangent" p1="(1.7062,1.5553)" p2="(-0.8052,3.0053)"
            stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4" dash="4 3"/>

      <vector at="ch-r1" tail="(0,0)" v="(1.1000,1.9053)" stroke="#666666" thickness="2"/>
      <vector at="ch-r2" tail="(0,0)" v="(-0.3820,2.1666)" stroke="#666666" thickness="2"/>

      <vector at="ch-dr" tail="(1.1000,1.9053)" v="(-1.4820,0.2613)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="ch-v" tail="(1.1000,1.9053)" v="(-1.2990,0.7500)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>

      <angle-marker font-size="18" at="ch-dtheta" points="((0.5,0.8660),(0,0),(-0.1736,0.9848))"
                    radius="52" stroke="black"><m>\Delta\theta</m></angle-marker>

      <point at="ch-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3"/>
      <point at="ch-P1" p="(1.1000,1.9053)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>
      <point at="ch-P2" p="(-0.3820,2.1666)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>

      <label font-size="18" at="ch-r1l" anchor="(1.32,1.98)" alignment="e" color="#666666">
      <m>\overrightarrow{r}(t)</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="ch-r2l" anchor="(-0.55,2.24)" alignment="nw" color="#666666">
      <m>\overrightarrow{r}(t+\Delta t)</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="ch-drl" anchor="(0.30,1.92)" alignment="s" color="#D55E00">
      <m>\Delta\overrightarrow{r}</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="ch-vl" anchor="(-0.10,2.70)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
      <m>\overrightarrow{v}(t)</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="ch-tl" anchor="(-0.86,3.06)" alignment="nw" color="#666666">
      <m>\text{tangent line}</m>
      </label>

      <!-- Plain \| bars, never \left\|...\right\|: a stretchy delimiter makes
           MathJax emit a nested <svg> inside the label, which browsers render
           flipped and stacked at the diagram origin. -->
      <label font-size="18" at="ch-note1" anchor="(0,-2.95)" alignment="c">
      <m>\|\Delta\overrightarrow{r}\| = 2r\sin\tfrac{\Delta\theta}{2} \approx r\,\Delta\theta</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="ch-note2" anchor="(0,-3.85)" alignment="c">
      <m>\Rightarrow\;\; v = \lim\limits_{\Delta t\to 0}\dfrac{\|\Delta\overrightarrow{r}\|}{\Delta t}
        = r\,\omega</m>
      </label>

      </coordinates>

      <annotations>
      <annotation ref="figure" text="Two position vectors of equal length r are drawn from the centre of a dashed circle, separated by a small angle delta theta. The chord delta r joins the tip of the first to the tip of the second. A dashed tangent line touches the circle at the first point, and the velocity arrow at that point lies along it. As delta t tends to zero the chord lines up with the tangent, and its length, two r sine of half delta theta, is approximately r times delta theta, giving speed equal to r times omega.">
        <annotation ref="ch-r1" text="The position vector at time t."/>
        <annotation ref="ch-r2" text="The position vector a short time delta t later, the same length but turned through delta theta."/>
        <annotation ref="ch-dr" text="The displacement delta r, the chord joining the two tips."/>
        <annotation ref="ch-v" text="The velocity at time t, tangent to the circle."/>
        <annotation ref="ch-tangent" text="The tangent line to the circle at the position at time t."/>
        <annotation ref="ch-dtheta" text="The small angle delta theta swept out during the time interval."/>
      </annotation>
      </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-angular-velocity-vector">
<title>Angular Velocity as a Vector</title>

<p>
So far the rate of turning has been a number, <m>d\theta/dt</m>. But a
rotation has a direction as well as a rate: the plane of the circle can be
tilted any which way, and the particle can go around it either way. Both
pieces of information can be carried by a single vector, provided we point it
along the axis of rotation rather than along the motion.
</p>

<p>
We always choose a right-handed coordinate system, with <m>\theta</m>
increasing in the counterclockwise direction when viewed from the positive
<m>z</m>-axis.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-angular-velocity">
<title>Angular Velocity Vector</title>
<statement>
<p>
For an object moving in a circle in the <m>xy</m>-plane about the origin, the
<term>angular velocity vector</term> is
<md xml:id="eq-angular-velocity" number="yes">
\overrightarrow{\omega} = \frac{d\theta}{dt}\,\hat{k} = \omega_z\,\hat{k},
</md>
directed along the axis of rotation. Its SI units are
<m>\text{rad}\cdot\text{s}^{-1}</m>, and the angular speed of
<xref ref="eq-angular-speed"/> is its magnitude,
<m>\omega = \left\|\overrightarrow{\omega}\right\| = \left|\omega_z\right|</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
The sign of <m>\omega_z</m> records which way the object goes around, and the
right-hand rule turns that sign into a direction in space: curl the fingers
of your right hand in the direction of the motion and your thumb points along
<m>\overrightarrow{\omega}</m>. If the particle circulates counterclockwise as seen from
above, then <m>\omega_z = d\theta/dt > 0</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{\omega}</m> points in
the <m>+\hat{k}</m>-direction; if it circulates clockwise, then
<m>\omega_z &lt; 0</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{\omega}</m> points in the
<m>-\hat{k}</m>-direction. The two cases are shown in
<xref ref="fig-angular-velocity-sign"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-angular-velocity-sign">
<caption>
The angular velocity vector points along the axis of rotation, in the
direction given by the right-hand rule: up the <m>z</m>-axis for
counterclockwise motion (left), down it for clockwise motion (right). Note
that <m>\overrightarrow{\omega}</m> is <em>not</em> tangent to the orbit — the velocity
<m>\overrightarrow{v}</m> is.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<description>
<p>
Two panels showing a particle on a circular orbit in the horizontal plane,
drawn in an oblique view with the z-axis vertical. In the left panel the
particle circulates counterclockwise as seen from above and the angular
velocity vector points up along the positive z-direction. In the right panel
the particle circulates clockwise and the angular velocity vector points down
along the negative z-direction. In both panels the velocity is tangent to the
orbit.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-angular-velocity-sign">
      <diagram dimensions="(710,372)" margins="14">
      <definition>orbA(t) = (2.2*cos(t), 0.75*sin(t))</definition>
      <definition>orbB(t) = (7.4 + 2.2*cos(t), 0.75*sin(t))</definition>
      <coordinates bbox="(-3.5,-3.7,11.2,4.0)">

      <!-- ============ PANEL A : counterclockwise, omega along +k ============ -->
      <line at="av-axA" p1="(0,-2.7)" p2="(0,3.05)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4"/>
      <parametric-curve at="av-orbA" function="orbA" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                        stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="2"/>
      <parametric-curve at="av-dirA" function="orbA" domain="(2.0,2.9)"
                        stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
      <vector at="av-rA" tail="(0,0)" v="(1.8021,-0.4302)" stroke="#666666" thickness="2"/>
      <vector at="av-vA" tail="(1.8021,-0.4302)" v="(1.1237,0.5471)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="av-wA" tail="(0,0)" v="(0,2.5)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="4"/>
      <point at="av-OA" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3"/>
      <point at="av-PA" p="(1.8021,-0.4302)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>

      <label font-size="18" at="av-wAl" anchor="(0.14,2.42)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
      <m>\overrightarrow{\omega}</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-zAl" anchor="(0.10,3.05)" alignment="e"><m>+z</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-OAl" anchor="(-0.12,-0.10)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-rAl" anchor="(0.98,-0.34)" alignment="s" color="#666666">
      <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-vAl" anchor="(3.00,0.18)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
      <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-topA" anchor="(-3.35,3.60)" alignment="e">
      <m>\omega_z = \dfrac{d\theta}{dt} > 0</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-botA" anchor="(-3.35,-3.25)" alignment="e">
      <m>\text{counterclockwise:}\;\; \overrightarrow{\omega} \text{ points along } +\hat{k}</m>
      </label>

      <!-- ============ PANEL B : clockwise, omega along -k ============ -->
      <line at="av-axB" p1="(7.4,-2.7)" p2="(7.4,3.05)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4"/>
      <parametric-curve at="av-orbB" function="orbB" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                        stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="2"/>
      <parametric-curve at="av-dirB" function="orbB" domain="(2.9,2.0)"
                        stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
